WEBVTT

NOTE
Disc 1, Scene 19h: Squall Confronts Edea
Duration: 30 seconds
Context: Squall confronts Edea inside Galbadia Garden's grand auditorium. After defeating her, Edea is freed from possession. The real threat is revealed — Sorceress Ultimecia from the future has been controlling Edea.
For blind and low-vision players using screen readers.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Squall enters a vast ceremonial hall within Galbadia Garden. The architecture is grand and imposing — high ceilings with arched supports, polished stone floors, and ornate columns. This is the auditorium, a place of authority and power.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
At the far end of the auditorium stands Edea, the Headmistress. She is imposing in her dark, flowing robes adorned with thorns and spikes. Her headdress is horned and menacing. Her eyes glow with power not entirely her own. Seifer stands nearby, a loyal knight in her presence. The air seems to crackle with sorcerous energy.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Squall and Edea engage in combat. Their battle is dramatic and intense. Edea unleashes powerful magic — ice storms, lightning strikes, and destructive spells tear through the auditorium. Stone columns crack and crumble. Squall counters with his gunblade, pressing his attack despite the magical onslaught.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
The battle reaches its climax. Squall lands a decisive blow. Edea staggers backward. The dark aura surrounding her flickers and fades. Her posture changes. The possessing force releases its grip on her mind.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Edea collapses. As she falls, her transformation completes. The dark robes fade. She reverts to her true self — Edea Kramer, kind headmistress and beloved wife of Headmaster Cid. Her eyes clear, no longer reflecting an alien malice. She looks confused and devastated by what her body has done while possessed. The revelation is staggering — someone else has been controlling her. Someone else orchestrated this entire invasion. Sorceress Ultimecia, a being from the future itself, has been the true enemy all along.